{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the earlier-version answer at head 585d8a08, tree f72d20dd, for request 4411ba40 and review request 6baa2a07. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for one sentence on the Change card. The server change holds.\n\n## The change I request\n\nThe Change card still tells a person the old route. `apps/views/src/change.ts:170` ends: \"For a finding on an earlier version, the first two tools also need answerAt from the change read, as at.\" After this head that's false, and it contradicts `docs/mcp.md` and the README, which both say that these tools need no `at`. You corrected the code comment at line 23 and not this string, and it's the sentence a person actually reads. Replace it with what the docs say, and add an assertion on the card's text to the real lane's case, with a control. This changes source, so the next head needs a new gate.\n\n## Your five points\n\n1. No, I don't find a listed act reaching an outcome it couldn't reach before. An explicit `at` already reached the same judgment for each one, so the rule grants nothing. What changes is who reaches it by accident: a caller with no `at` used to stop at `bad-intent` and now gets a precise refusal, or an acceptance if the act's guards allow a final subject. I read one: `revoke-key` guards the key's own state and the last admin, and has no guard on the `member` it reads through `via`. So the guards decide, as they should. You list the other acts and say that no test runs them, and I didn't trace them all. I accept keeping the rule general, because `service.ts` names no application.\n\n2. No. The read uses the same `#reads.read` and session as the caller's other reads, so it reaches only what `items` already can. The item comes from a slot of a subject, and the result is used only for its revision. A refusal maps to `forbidden`, `too-large` or `unavailable`, with the scope the caller already named. A caller on signed reads gets `read` with `forbidden` where it used to get `bad-intent`, which is fine.\n\n3. Yes, for what can move it. `effects.ts:241` and `:360` refuse an effect on a final item, and `judge.ts:304` refuses a transition. The judge does allow comments, and I didn't trace whether a comment moves a revision. If anything did, the act is refused as `revision-moved`, so a wrong number fails closed and signs nothing that lands. Say \"an act with a stale number is refused\" beside that comment, and it's safe either way.\n\n4. Yes, that's enough. The refusal comes from the real scope, and the test reaches the real `stopCall` path. Blocking sessions stops the agent earlier, so stripping the reader is the honest way in, and the note says so. F9 fails on the swallowed refusal.\n\n5. Yes. Both seals move, and `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 of 10.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The gate at 6a2f581a, tree 784e987d, exits 0: Plain 3,241 with one skip, Native 907 with two skips. From there to this head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` of ada88648 and this head gives this head's tree.\n- The evidence verifies. All 6 manifest rows match, the directory holds 113 files with none writable, and `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 04a7c0d8.\n- `controls/summary.json` shows F1 to F11 each failing by an assertion, and each names a claim. I read those rows and did not rerun them.\n- I ran `change-view.scope.test.ts` and `shape.test.ts` once, and both pass. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` finds 780 links with 0 broken, `check-commit-messages` checks 3 with 0 flagged, and the worktree stayed clean.\n\nNo Worker or room has run this, and no independent second read ran.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved in substance. The server reads what the scope already requires, and the scope stays the judge.\n- Security: approved in substance. No authority widens.\n- Simplification: `subjectsOf` removes a duplicate walk, and `viaLeft` is small. No objection.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:58